+//! The command line as an interface another program consumes.
+//!
+//! `jobqd` is a process a supervisor starts; `jobqc` is a tool a *script* runs, and a script is a
+//! customer with no patience and no imagination: it reads `$?`, it reads stdout, it reads stderr
+//! into a log, and it cannot answer a question. Everything in this module follows from that one
+//! sentence, and the four rules it produces are the step's contract:
+//!
+//! * **stdout is the answer, stderr is the diagnosis.** A command that succeeded prints its answer
+//!   on stdout and nothing on stderr; a command that failed prints *nothing* on stdout and a
+//!   sentence on stderr. A script that pipes stdout into a parser must never be handed half a
+//!   document plus a warning, which is why the two streams are decided here, as one value
+//!   ([`Outcome`]), instead of at five call sites that each remember the rule.
+//! * **one exit code per failure class.** `0` answered, `2` the call was wrong, `3` the thing asked
+//!   about is not there, `4` the store refused, `1` this deployment is broken.
+//!   `jobq/3.3-library-errors` wrote that table and [`crate::engine::Error::exit_code`] is it, so
+//!   this module never invents a code: it hands the failure over.
+//! * **`--json` never changes the exit code.** It changes what the answer *looks like* and nothing
+//!   else — the refusal path does not consult it — so a script can add `--json` to a working
+//!   command line and keep its error handling.
+//! * **nothing prompts.** Every input arrives as a flag or on standard input, and a call that is
+//!   missing one is a usage error naming what is missing. A command that waits for a human is a
+//!   command that hangs in CI, and the hang is the failure mode nobody debugs.
+//!
+//! The grammar is small on purpose — `submit`, `inspect`, `list`, `usage` — and it grows where a
+//! step needs it (`jobq/3.16-dlq-tooling` adds the two commands that act on a dead letter). What
+//! does not grow is the shape: a subcommand, its flags, one answer, one code.
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! jobqc submit --queue signup --payload '{"to":"ada@example.com"}'   →  queued 4f1c…
+//! jobqc list --queue signup --json                                   →  [{"id":"4f1c…",…}]
+//! ```
+//!
+//! The text shapes are one line per thing, and the payload inside them is compact JSON on that
+//! line: a payload that carried a newline would break `wc -l` as a way of counting jobs, so the
+//! encoding is what keeps "one line per job" true rather than a promise about payloads. The JSON
+//! shapes are one document, `[]` for an empty answer rather than nothing at all — a script that has
+//! to special-case "no output" is a script that gets it wrong once and then stops trusting the
+//! tool.
+//!
+//! # Where the engine is
+//!
+//! [`run`] answers with an [`Outcome`] and the binary writes it, so the contract is checkable
+//! without a process: the step's spec calls this function *and* runs the real binary. The store, on
+//! the other hand, is not this module's: the doors are the engine's ([`Engine`], and the per-tenant
+//! handle it hands out), and the settings that say which backend to open are the daemon's
+//! (`crate::service::open_store`). A command line that opened a pool itself would be a second
+//! assembly of this crate, and therefore a second thing that can disagree with the daemon about
+//! which queue it is talking to — the boundary `jobq/3.1-engine-boundary` drew.
+//!
+//! # What is left to write
+//!
+//! Everything between the command line and that value: which subcommand was asked for, which flags
+//! it takes, what each answer looks like, and which failures are the caller's. The usage text below
+//! is the grammar — it is the interface a script reads and the contract this file keeps — so it is
+//! given; the parser, the renderers and the two `Outcome` constructors are not, because "stdout is
+//! the answer, stderr is the diagnosis" is what this step is about, and a constructor that already
+//! did it would be the answer.
+
+use std::io::Read;
+
+/// The usage text: what the tool is, every command it has, and the codes it leaves behind.
+pub const USAGE: &str = "\
+usage: jobqc [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
+
+COMMANDS:
+    submit                  queue one job and print its identity
+    inspect <ID>            print one job
+    list                    print the jobs this engine can see
+    usage                   print what the queues have done
+    help                    print this help
+
+OPTIONS:
+    --json                  print the answer as one JSON value on stdout
+    --config <FILE>         read settings from a TOML file (the same file jobqd reads)
+    --help, -h              print this help and exit
+    --version               print the version and exit
+
+COMMAND OPTIONS:
+    submit  --queue <NAME> --payload <JSON> | --stdin [--priority <N>] [--max-attempts <N>]
+    inspect <ID>
+    list    [--queue <NAME>] [--state <STATE>] [--limit <N>]
+
+Settings are read from the file given by --config and overridden by the environment, where every
+setting is spelled `JOBQ__SECTION__KEY` (for example `JOBQ__STORE__URL`, which is how a script
+points this tool at the same queue the daemon drains).
+
+Exit codes: 0 the command answered, 2 the call was wrong, 3 the thing asked about is not there,
+4 the store refused, 1 this deployment is broken.
+";
+
+/// What the process would have written, and the code it would have ended with.
+///
+/// One value rather than three writes, and that is the shape of the contract: "stdout is the
+/// answer, stderr is the diagnosis, the code is the class" is a decision about *this* value, so a
+/// test can assert it directly and a subprocess test asserts the same decision from outside. The
+/// binary that writes these bytes is a handful of lines because there is nothing left to decide.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Outcome {
+    /// What the process would exit with.
+    code: u8,
+    /// What standard output would have received.
+    stdout: String,
+    /// What standard error would have received.
+    stderr: String,
+}
+
+impl Outcome {
+    /// The bytes standard output would have received.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn stdout(&self) -> &str {
+        &self.stdout
+    }
+
+    /// The bytes standard error would have received.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn stderr(&self) -> &str {
+        &self.stderr
+    }
+
+    /// The code the process would have ended with.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn code(&self) -> u8 {
+        self.code
+    }
+}
+
+/// Run one command line: `args` without the program name, the environment the process was given,
+/// and its standard input.
+///
+/// Synchronous on purpose. A command that talks to a store needs a runtime, and the runtime is
+/// built inside that branch — so `--help` answers on a machine where a runtime cannot even start,
+/// and a program that wants this grammar from its own code does not have to be an async one to get
+/// it.
+///
+/// Nothing here panics and nothing here exits: every path a caller can take — a usage error, a
+/// store that is not reachable, a job that is not there — comes back as an [`Outcome`].
+#[must_use]
+pub fn run(
+    args: impl IntoIterator<Item = String>,
+    env: &[(String, String)],
+    input: &mut dyn Read,
+) -> Outcome {
+    todo!(
+        "jobq/3.15-cli-contract: the grammar, the two streams and the exit code — `USAGE` is the \
+         interface and everything else is yours"
+    )
+}
